## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer mechanisms, wakeup latency, and concrete data structures suitable for embedded or real-time operating systems.

## Key Concepts
- Timers with absolute expiry and optional periodicity
- Heap-based scheduling for efficient expiry checks
- Interrupt-safe wakeups and drift prevention
- Latency testing with mixed workloads

## Code Example
```javascript
function timer_add(heap, expiry, period, task) {
  push(heap, { expiry, period, task });
  siftUp(heap, heap.length - 1);
}

function timer_interrupt(now_ms) {
  while (heap.length > 0 && heap[0].expiry <= now_ms) {
    const timer = popMin(heap);
    timer.task();
    if (timer.period > 0) {
      timer.expiry = now_ms + timer.period;
      push(heap, timer);
    }
  }
}
```

## Testing Considerations
Run mixed CPU-bound and timer-heavy workloads simultaneously. Measure wake latency (actual vs. expected wake time) and jitter (variance in wake intervals) to validate drift prevention and real-time guarantees.

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding safe concurrent data sharing is fundamental for OS-level IPC. This task tests practical use of atomics, memory ordering, and race testing.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffer basics, head/tail invariants
- Atomic operations: CAS, fetch_add, acquire/release
- Memory ordering and visibility on multi-core CPUs
- Overflow handling and backpressure

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t head;
  _Atomic uint32_t tail;
  int slots[256];
} ring_t;

bool enqueue(ring_t *r, int val){
  uint32_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h >= 256) return false; // full
  r->slots[t & 255] = val;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(ring_t *r, int *out){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return false; // empty
  *out = r->slots[h & 255];
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

